            ":question/id": "d8d64dd8-1408-5c35-8e37-ca364a99f3e7",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "q-67627",
            ":question/problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{x})=$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {4}^{x} &=-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={4}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/db/db8c8ab9423f9c56a6eb4c4602bb8c2e162e07c9a2e4ac99f7670343146ce13a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{4}^{x}=\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"
