            ":question/id": "463b4fa2-ca0f-5476-9910-58395362f5e7",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "q-47083",
            ":question/problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{(-4x-3)}=$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{(-4x-3)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-4x}\\cdot {e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({e}^{-4})}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/a1/a15e97dbbca01e1482ffdbeb3872c432af38a6d8da1a60f871f07614f4a100d7.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}$ does not affect the sign of the limit. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}\\right)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{{e}^{4}}\\right)}^{x}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{(-4x-3)}=\\infty .\n$$"
          },
