            ":question/id": "5d484ecf-3364-5e2b-92f9-7ca62c79da9f",
            ":question/math-academy-id": "q-99509",
            ":question/problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x-1}=$",
            ":question/worked-solution": "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{3}^{2x}\\cdot {3}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{({3}^{2})}^{x}\\cdot {3}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{9}^{x}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{9}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={9}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/d4/d4023b19a813658c0d6e395497646fa8dbf4d76139c54c2a363fddaeb1baf823.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{9}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{9}^{x}=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$"
          },
